From 23a1fb658bdbea8c411fc7411eb98ec426963e0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: alexander-abramovich <alexander.abramovich.18@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 16:18:25 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] P2 prototype: the change classifier names its verdict, and
 one orchestrator runs the chain
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The reach map answered an exit code and nothing else, so the push gate knew how wide
to run but nobody could see WHY, and nothing said what review the change owed. This
prototype adds both, and keeps every existing output line byte-identical so the tools
that parse them are untouched.

The classifier now answers four verdicts in words, on lines beginning VERDICT:

  CROSS-CUTTING  a changed file is a declared cross-cut surface. Asked FIRST, ahead of
                 every class, and wins outright — a surface a change to which reaches
                 everything is not classified further. Review: fresh-record.
  UNSURE         a changed file sits in two classes at once (a duplicated mapping the
                 config must fix) or in none. Full path, and the review is computed
                 from review_policy.unsure against the whole changed set.
  LOCAL-PROSE    the exit-0 path. Review: lints-only.
  LOCAL-SCOPED   the exit-2 path. Review: riders-only.

Exit codes 0/2/1 are unchanged, as is every SCOPED and OK (reach) line gate b parses.

The surfaces and the review answers are DECLARED in guardrails.config.json under two
new objects, cross_cut and review_policy, and read from there — the shell holds no
rider list to drift. One config reader serves all three lists: it walks a nested key
path and expands an "@key" entry to the config's own top-level key of that name, so
user_facing_globs is referenced once rather than copied into a second place.

guardrails/pre-push takes a chain lock before any gate runs. Two chains deciding on
one tree can each read a state the other is mid-way through changing. The lock is a
noclobber file, so the create is atomic and the loser never mistakes the race for a
win; a crashed chain leaves the file behind, so the holder is checked for life and a
dead pid is recovered out loud rather than blocking every future push on a corpse.
A trap releases the lock on every ordinary exit path.

Four nets hold the declarations honest: the class lists carry no path written into two
of them, every cross-cut trigger is a real path in the tree, review_policy answers
exactly the four verdicts with allowed words and no dangling "@" reference, and the
two shell scripts hand-list no trigger. That last net forbids the LIST SHAPE — an
array, a case pattern, or two triggers enumerated on one line — not the mention, since
pre-push legitimately passes PRODUCT_SPEC.md and TEST_MATRIX.md to gates as arguments.

Prototype only: no rebuilds map (the rebuild commands stay in guardrails/README.md),
and nothing is wired into the gate chain's decisions yet.

